---
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { pickAndPlaceSource, pickAndPlaceValidate } from '../data/examples';
import { CORE_REPO, LICENSE_NAME, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="RoboLang: a common language for robotic tasks"
  description="RoboLang is an open-source task description language for robots. Write tasks, validate their structure and inspect a versioned intermediate representation."
>
  <section slot="head" class="hero on-ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ul class="chips" aria-label="Project facts">
        <li><span class="chip"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true"></span>Developer preview</span></li>
        <li><span class="chip">Open source</span></li>
        <li><span class="chip">Language {versions.language}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h1>A common language for robotic tasks.</h1>
      <div class="signal-rules" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="lede">
            Write, validate and inspect structured robot tasks. Built for developers and AI systems.
            No hardware required.
          </p>
          <p>
            RoboLang describes <em>what</em> a robot should do in a small, typed, human-readable text
            format. Today it ships read-only developer tools that check a task file and show exactly
            what it says, before anything is connected to a robot.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
            <a class="button secondary" href={CORE_REPO}>View on GitHub</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cropframe">
          <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={pickAndPlaceSource} label="examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
          <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceValidate} label="$ robolang validate examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="specstrip">
        <div><dt>Distribution</dt><dd>{versions.distribution}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>{versions.language}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>IR schema</dt><dd>{versions.irSchema}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Python</dt><dd>{versions.pythonMinimum}+</dd></div>
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>{LICENSE_NAME}</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <div class="section-body prose">
      <p>
        Robot tasks are often written as ad hoc scripts or free-form text. A typo, a missing argument
        or a name that was never declared is found late, usually when something is already running.
        That gets worse when the task text is produced by a language model instead of a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        RoboLang's purpose is to give a task a precise written form with a published grammar, so that
        tools can reject a malformed task with a clear message and hand a well-formed one to other
        software as structured data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What works today</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="prose">
        Distribution {versions.distribution} installs one command, <code>robolang</code>, with two
        read-only subcommands. It needs Python {versions.pythonMinimum} or newer and has no other
        dependencies.
      </p>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">A / Language</p>
          <h3>Write</h3>
          <p>
            Language {versions.language} has a formal grammar: tasks with typed parameters, <code
              >@safety</code
            > annotations, a <code>pre</code> block and a <code>plan</code> of six statement kinds.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/reference/">Language reference</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">B / Validator</p>
          <h3>Validate</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang validate</code> checks syntax, names, types and values, and reports each
            problem as <code>file:line:column</code> with a stable diagnostic code.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#invalid">See diagnostics</a></p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <p class="syslabel">C / IR</p>
          <h3>Inspect</h3>
          <p>
            <code>robolang inspect</code> shows what a valid file describes, as text or as JSON in IR
            schema {versions.irSchema}, for use by other tools.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/examples/#json-ir">See the JSON IR</a></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 id="limitations">What it does not do</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="prose">
        These limits are deliberate for the developer preview. Please read them before you start.
      </p>
      <dl class="readout">
        <div>
          <dt>Execution <span class="status status--off">Not available</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not execute tasks.</strong> The supported package contains no code that can
            run a task or move a robot, and there is no command that does.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Safety enforcement <span class="status status--off">Not available</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not enforce safety.</strong>
            <code>@safety</code> limits and <code>pre</code> conditions are parsed and checked as text.
            They are not evaluated or enforced by anything. A file that validates is well formed; it is
            not thereby safe to run.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>ROS 2 <span class="status status--off">Not supported</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It has no ROS 2 integration.</strong> The core repository keeps an early, never
            validated ROS 2 adapter as historical source. It is not installed and not supported.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>AI generation <span class="status status--off">Not included</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It does not generate tasks with AI.</strong> The language is designed to be easy
            for language models to write, but no model or planner is included.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Release <span class="status status--on">Alpha</span></dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>It is an alpha.</strong> It is installed from a clone of the repository and is not
            published on PyPI. Details may change between versions.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="prose">
        Execution, interoperability and AI-assisted authoring are research directions, described on
        the <a href="/roadmap/">Vision &amp; Roadmap</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <div class="section-body prose">
      <p>
        Robotics developers, ROS 2 engineers and researchers who want to experiment with a checkable
        task format, including as a target for tasks written by language models. Feedback on the
        language and the tools is the main thing the project needs right now.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/get-started/">Install and try it</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/contribute/">Give feedback</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
